# Mutation plan for the local file backend and the 21 routes on it.
#
#   scripts/mutate-batch.sh scripts/mutations/file-backend.plan
#
# Three layers, and they fail differently.
#
# `serve_content.rs` is a port of `http.ServeContent`, whose decisions are almost all *deletions*
# — a 304 without a Content-Type, a 416 without a Cache-Control, an `Accept-Ranges` that must not
# appear on the 416 because the line that sets it is below the range check. A mutation that adds a
# header back is the shape a reader would actually write, so most of the first block does exactly
# that.
#
# `filestore.rs` is a port of `os` semantics, where the two mutations that matter are the ENOENT/
# ENOTDIR split (the intuitive reading is the wrong one, and the oracle caught it) and the listing
# prefix.
#
# The `api` block mutates the four places the three byte routes disagree with each other, plus the
# two JSON encoders whose only difference is a trailing newline, plus the `me` alias — which is a
# real divergence this session shipped and then fixed, so the mutation reproduces a bug that
# actually existed.
#
# The first run was 38 mutations, 33 caught, and two real survivors — both fixture gaps of the
# same shape, a corpus that never reached a boundary:
#
#   * `serve-no-overlap-starts-one-late` loosens `parseRange`'s `start >= size` to `start > size`.
#     Every unsatisfiable range in the corpus began *well* past the end, where the two readings
#     agree; the case that separates them is a range beginning at byte `size` exactly, which is a
#     416 and not an empty 206. Added as `range_starts_exactly_at_the_end`, with `range_last_byte`
#     one byte earlier so the boundary is pinned from both sides.
# A third survivor was a **bad mutation rather than a fixture gap**, and is recorded because the
# distinction is the whole value of the exercise: `serve-path-escape-leaves-the-semicolon`
# originally deleted `b';'` from the escaped arm of `should_escape_in_path_segment`, which is a
# no-op — `;` then falls through to the catch-all `_ => true` and is escaped anyway. The mutation
# now moves it to the *unescaped* arm, which is the change a reader would actually make.
#
# It did nonetheless expose a real gap on the way: no filename in the corpus contained a `;`, so
# `url.PathEscape` was only ever exercised on space, quote, slash and non-ASCII — four of the ten
# characters whose treatment differs. `filename_with_sub_delims` carries all ten.
#
# Both controls survived, which is the verdict they must give.
#
# The last two lines are the no-op controls and must SURVIVE.

